1
00:00:00,623 --> 00:00:02,494
Welcome to CLM crew training.

2
00:00:02,494 --> 00:00:10,161
In about six minutes you'll learn to start the service, ask it questions, and train a classifier on your own labelled examples.

3
00:00:10,161 --> 00:00:16,620
You'll see how to judge whether it is good enough to use, and how to export it to another machine or to S3.

4
00:00:17,425 --> 00:00:19,188
CLM is a fast classifier.

5
00:00:19,188 --> 00:00:24,282
A frozen NVIDIA Nemotron model reads each text once and turns it into numbers.

6
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:27,155
Two small networks on top learn your labels.

7
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:34,339
Training takes seconds to minutes, answers come back in tens of milliseconds, and nothing leaves your machine.

8
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:40,151
Use it for routing, tagging and triage, where the labels are fixed and you have examples.

9
00:00:40,151 --> 00:00:44,461
For open questions or writing, use a large language model instead.

10
00:00:45,266 --> 00:00:51,353
To start it, clone the repository, open Claude Code in the folder, and type: start the service.

11
00:00:51,353 --> 00:00:57,184
The first time, it installs everything and downloads the models, which takes a few minutes.

12
00:00:57,184 --> 00:01:03,719
After that it starts in seconds, and prints the address to open: localhost:8700.

13
00:01:05,124 --> 00:01:06,540
This is the playground.

14
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:10,664
At the top, a switch between asking questions and training a model.

15
00:01:10,664 --> 00:01:15,527
On the right, the status light: green means the service and its encoder are up.

16
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:22,913
Click it to see every loaded model, what each one answers, how accurate it was on its test set, and which one is in use.

17
00:01:22,913 --> 00:01:25,252
The arrow beside a model downloads it.

18
00:01:26,265 --> 00:01:29,721
On the left is the state: the text you want to classify.

19
00:01:29,721 --> 00:01:31,388
Below it are the questions.

20
00:01:31,388 --> 00:01:37,807
A yes or no question, a pick-one question with its options described in plain words, and a rating scale.

21
00:01:37,807 --> 00:01:42,128
Choose a model, here the shipped typed decisions model, and press Run.

22
00:01:42,729 --> 00:01:44,737
Each question gets its own card.

23
00:01:44,737 --> 00:01:49,819
The highlighted bar is the model's answer, and the percentage is its probability.

24
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:55,278
Treat those numbers as a ranking, not a guarantee, until the model has been calibrated.

25
00:01:55,278 --> 00:02:01,928
The JSON tab shows exactly what came back, and the Python tab gives a developer the same request as code.

26
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:05,969
Now the main event: training your own classifier.

27
00:02:05,969 --> 00:02:07,654
Switch to Train a model.

28
00:02:07,654 --> 00:02:13,833
Four steps on one page: add examples, describe the labels, train, and check the results.

29
00:02:14,437 --> 00:02:15,479
First, the data.

30
00:02:15,479 --> 00:02:20,559
You need a file with one column of text and one column with the correct label.

31
00:02:20,559 --> 00:02:22,839
A spreadsheet saved as CSV works.

32
00:02:22,839 --> 00:02:25,639
Aim for at least thirty examples per label.

33
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:33,194
If several rows come from the same customer or case, include that ID column: you'll use it to keep the test honest.

34
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:40,717
We'll use the bundled sample: four hundred synthetic support conversations in five categories.

35
00:02:40,717 --> 00:02:45,678
CLM finds the text and label columns by itself; you can change them here.

36
00:02:45,678 --> 00:02:48,588
Group by keeps one customer's rows together.

37
00:02:48,588 --> 00:02:54,673
The preview highlights the two columns, and the chips show how many examples each label has.

38
00:02:54,673 --> 00:02:57,716
A label with very few examples gets a warning.

39
00:02:58,325 --> 00:02:59,675
Step two matters most.

40
00:02:59,675 --> 00:03:02,131
The model doesn't read your label names.

41
00:03:02,131 --> 00:03:06,121
It reads your descriptions, and compares every text against them.

42
00:03:06,121 --> 00:03:10,725
So describe each label with the words that actually appear in its examples.

43
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:15,329
Watch what happens with a weak description: the page warns you as you type.

44
00:03:15,329 --> 00:03:17,048
Let's put the good one back.

45
00:03:17,653 --> 00:03:18,366
Step three.

46
00:03:18,366 --> 00:03:23,944
Give the model a name, and check how the data is split, here the file's own test rows.

47
00:03:23,944 --> 00:03:25,111
Press Train model.

48
00:03:25,111 --> 00:03:29,975
The encoder reads every text once, then the small networks train in rounds.

49
00:03:29,975 --> 00:03:36,655
The line is accuracy on a check set the model never trains on, and the dashed line is where it started.

50
00:03:36,655 --> 00:03:39,833
Training stops by itself when it stops improving.

51
00:03:41,344 --> 00:03:43,056
Step four: is it any good?

52
00:03:43,056 --> 00:03:47,797
The headline is how many of a hundred unseen conversations it got right.

53
00:03:47,797 --> 00:03:55,107
Below it, two baselines: the base model without any training, and simply always guessing the most common label.

54
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:57,609
A useful model must clearly beat both.

55
00:03:57,609 --> 00:04:03,536
And mind the margin: with a hundred test examples, a few points either way is just chance.

56
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:07,114
Try it on a new message before you trust it.

57
00:04:07,114 --> 00:04:09,413
Paste any text and press Classify.

58
00:04:09,413 --> 00:04:12,319
Here, a double charge is routed to billing.

59
00:04:12,923 --> 00:04:15,526
The model is already live on this server.

60
00:04:15,526 --> 00:04:22,510
Open in playground loads it with its question, and the copy buttons give developers ready-made Python or curl.

61
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:25,430
It is saved, so it comes back after a restart.

62
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:29,133
To take the model somewhere else, export it.

63
00:04:29,133 --> 00:04:32,575
Download gives you one archive for this computer.

64
00:04:32,575 --> 00:04:39,883
Or type a folder on the server, or an S3 location like s3 colon slash slash my bucket, and press Export.

65
00:04:39,883 --> 00:04:47,471
You get three files: the model, a card that records its encoder, its labels and its accuracy, and checksums.

66
00:04:47,471 --> 00:04:53,654
Another CLM server loads the model with one flag, as long as it runs the same encoder.

67
00:04:53,654 --> 00:04:57,448
From Claude Code, just say: export ticket topic to S3.

68
00:04:58,052 --> 00:05:03,053
Back in the playground, the new model answers the same question on any text you give it.

69
00:05:04,757 --> 00:05:07,583
With your own data, a few habits matter most.

70
00:05:07,583 --> 00:05:09,844
Use labels that people have checked.

71
00:05:09,844 --> 00:05:12,921
Group rows by customer, so the test stays honest.

72
00:05:12,921 --> 00:05:18,260
Describe each label with the words its examples use, and avoid catch-alls like other.

73
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:25,043
If the model doesn't clearly beat both baselines, improve the descriptions or the data before anything else.

74
00:05:25,043 --> 00:05:27,555
And retrain whenever your labels change.

75
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:29,658
Finally, governance.

76
00:05:29,658 --> 00:05:34,589
The models shipped with the repository are demos, trained on synthetic data.

77
00:05:34,589 --> 00:05:44,451
Before a model makes real decisions, it needs human-labelled data, calibrated probabilities, and the new model checklist in the Model Governance folder.

78
00:05:44,451 --> 00:05:48,409
The user guide covers everything in this video, step by step.

79
00:05:48,409 --> 00:05:49,707
Thanks for watching.
